      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rosine was named after the pen name of poet Jennie Taylor McHenry, wife of founder Henry D.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosine%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Bill Monroe, the Father of Bluegrass, was born in Rosine, where he developed the sound that would influence generations of musicians worldwide.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitolia.com/rosine-travel-guide-V102109",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Every Saturday night, the historic Rosine Barn hosts a free bluegrass show called the Rosine Barn Jamboree, drawing local and regional bands.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitolia.com/rosine-travel-guide-V102109",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rosine General Store and Barn, a registered historic place, preserves early bluegrass music history and serves as a museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosine%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Louisville and Paducah railroad once ran through Rosine, linking the town to regional trade routes.",
